pital you know card is  overflowing  with with beds beds outside the hospital  uh and he writes there's nothing here  but death and destruction  just a few miles away in little rock  arkansas  the arkansas gazette princess big  headlines spanish influenza  same old chills and grip and  and you know it's just total dismissive  just like  it was ordinary influenza uh you know  there are a couple of exceptions  uh there's a small city in wisconsin  where the uh  newspaper started to print the truth and  the army started to institute  prosecution against them uh  under that law i mentioned a little  while ago  uh although the prosecution was dropped  as the pandemic proceeded uh  san francisco is is a  uh was pretty exceptional and san  francisco  uh which got hit pretty late actually  the uh in you know most cities  say they're saying things like this is  ordinary influence by another name  san francisco the mayor the business  leaders  trade union leaders and the medical  community all got together  signed a joint statement huge typeface  in the newspapers saying where mass can  save your life  uh what was the matter within 18. this  is in 1918  where a mass can save a life say you  save your life  so that is a very different message than  saying this is ordinary influenza  by another name which was the most  common treatment of it in the press  or a third example in phoenix  they wrote a little bit about it when  the disease erupted in boston which was  the first place in the united states to  get hit by the  disease game and waves and the second  wave was  the deadly wave so  when it's in boston they write a little  bit about it in phoenix  when it's in new orleans they write less  about it in phoenix  when it's actually in phoenix it  disappears from the newspapers  not a word initially because  again they didn't want any bad news out  there that might hurt the war effort  eventually when it was impossible to  ignore  uh the phoenix paper did start writing  about it but  again initially to  keep morale up and so forth and so on uh  they ignored it you know in other  newspapers they simply they  didn't even print names of the people  who died  and you know all of this had  a impact on the public and it wasn't  what they wanted  instead of keeping morale up it hurt  morale more than  the truth would have uh because people  knew again their neighbors are dying  in some cases the bodies are lying in  the house for days because there's no  one to get take it out  and they ca and people knew this was  happening  all around them they knew that they were  being lied to by the  by the media and by the public officials  and all that did was alienate them and  and  caused them to feel that they had no one  they could rely on  except for themselves uh and  you know it's sort of split society  apart john for you  did you ever sit down and have a  conversation about this with your  grandparents  i'm assuming your parents are probably  born in the 20s  or or late teens actually my mother just  died at 102 and she was born in 1918.  oh and you got to be kidding me wow  but when i first started working on the  book  you know my grandparents were dead uh  but i had  anon who was considerably older than my  mother  uh and when i told her that i was  working on the book  i mean she sort of like grabbed her to  her chest and said  oh my god then you know that's the only  time she ever saw her father  to cry that was because a couple  directly across the street who had a  couple of kids  you know both of them died uh leaving  several orphans which was not uncommon  because as i said earlier the  18 to 45 is like the peak a  age at which people were dying so there  were a lot of orphans created  so if you like this sort of short clip  from an interview i did click over here  to watch the entire  interview and please don't forget to  subscribe to the channel take care  everybody bye  [Music]  you

